# Template for the cert-manager DNS-01 Cloudflare secret.
#
# How to use (one-time setup, ~2 min):
#
# 1. Get a CF API token with these scopes (or reuse cloudflare-tf-token
#    which already has them):
#      Zone — Zone:Read    (any zone)
#      Zone — DNS:Edit     (etherport.net)
#
# 2. Copy this template to the real file:
#      cp cloudflare-credentials.sops.yaml.template cloudflare-credentials.sops.yaml
#
# 3. Open with SOPS (it will decrypt + open in $EDITOR, you replace the
#    placeholder, save, SOPS re-encrypts on close):
#      sops platform/kubernetes/traefik/cloudflare-credentials.sops.yaml
#
# 4. Replace REPLACE_WITH_CF_TOKEN with the actual token. Save + quit.
#
# 5. Add to kustomization.yaml + commit:
#      sed -i '' '/route53-credentials\.sops\.yaml/a\
#      - cloudflare-credentials.sops.yaml' platform/kubernetes/traefik/kustomization.yaml
#      git add platform/kubernetes/traefik/{cloudflare-credentials.sops.yaml,kustomization.yaml}
#
# 6. Update clusterissuer-letsencrypt.yaml — swap route53 solver for cloudflare
#    (the new spec is already in the commit alongside this file — see
#    clusterissuer-letsencrypt.yaml's git diff)
#
# 7. Commit + push. Flux reconciles within ~1min.
#
# 8. Force a renewal to validate before the natural one fires Saturday:
#      kubectl -n traefik delete certificaterequest \
#        $(kubectl -n traefik get certificaterequest -o name | head -1)
#      # cert-manager re-issues the cert immediately
#
# Until step 7 lands, the shortlived wildcard cert renewal fails
# (Route53 zone is deleted). Current cert expires 2026-06-01T13:24:20Z.
#
# The route53-credentials.sops.yaml in this directory can be deleted
# after cutover is verified (and the K8s secret cleaned up in both
# traefik + cert-manager namespaces).
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
    name: cloudflare-credentials
    namespace: cert-manager
type: Opaque
stringData:
    api-token: REPLACE_WITH_CF_TOKEN
